2414 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (The chart above shows a typical sell-side liquidity scenario where price briefly drops below a support level before reversing direction. Support levels tend to attract retail traders looking to buy, expecting the price to bounce back. To protect their positions, these traders place stop-loss orders just below the support level, which are essentially sell orders. As a result, a large pool of sell orders accumulates just beneath the support zone.)
/E (The chart above shows a typical sell-side liquidity scenario where price briefly drops below a support level before reversing direction. Support levels tend to attract retail traders looking to buy, expecting the price to bounce back. To protect their positions, these traders place stop-loss orders just below the support level, which are essentially sell orders. As a result, a large pool of sell orders accumulates just beneath the support zone.)
/Pg 76 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2415 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Institutional traders, or "smart money," are aware of this pattern and exploit it. By pushing the price below the support level, they trigger these stop-loss orders, effectively creating sell-side liquidity. As the price drops, more retail traders enter short positions, adding to the liquidity pool.)
/E (Institutional traders, or "smart money," are aware of this pattern and exploit it. By pushing the price below the support level, they trigger these stop-loss orders, effectively creating sell-side liquidity. As the price drops, more retail traders enter short positions, adding to the liquidity pool.)
/Pg 76 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
