2515 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (                        Unlike retail traders, who often react to price movements, institutions actively create and manipulate liquidity to execute their trades efficiently. Their primary goal is to enter and exit large positions without causing excessive market impact. To achieve this, they use strategies like stop-hunting, fake breakouts, liquidity sweeps, and order absorption.)
/E (                        Unlike retail traders, who often react to price movements, institutions actively create and manipulate liquidity to execute their trades efficiently. Their primary goal is to enter and exit large positions without causing excessive market impact. To achieve this, they use strategies like stop-hunting, fake breakouts, liquidity sweeps, and order absorption.)
/Pg 82 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
2516 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (1. Stop-Hunting: Triggering Retail Stop-Losses)
/E (1. Stop-Hunting: Triggering Retail Stop-Losses)
/Pg 82 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2517 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (One of the most common ways institutions exploit liquidity is by triggering stop-loss orders placed by retail traders. Many retail traders place their stop-losses above resistance levels in a short trade or below support levels in a long trade. Institutions are aware of these predictable stop-loss placements and intentionally push prices into these zones to generate liquidity.)
/E (One of the most common ways institutions exploit liquidity is by triggering stop-loss orders placed by retail traders. Many retail traders place their stop-losses above resistance levels in a short trade or below support levels in a long trade. Institutions are aware of these predictable stop-loss placements and intentionally push prices into these zones to generate liquidity.)
/Pg 82 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
2518 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (For example, imagine a stock has a strong support level at $100, where many traders have placed buy orders and stop-losses just below at $98. Institutions may drive the price down to $97, triggering these stop-losses. This forced selling provides institutions with enough liquidity to enter their own large buy orders. Once they have accumulated enough buy orders at a lower price, they push the price back up, leaving retail traders behind.)
/E (For example, imagine a stock has a strong support level at $100, where many traders have placed buy orders and stop-losses just below at $98. Institutions may drive the price down to $97, triggering these stop-losses. This forced selling provides institutions with enough liquidity to enter their own large buy orders. Once they have accumulated enough buy orders at a lower price, they push the price back up, leaving retail traders behind.)
/Pg 82 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
