/E (In the chart above, we see a clear example of a liquidity sweep occurring at a well-defined support level. The blue shaded area highlights a zone where price has reacted multiple times in the past, forming a level that many traders recognize as support. Naturally, this becomes an area where traders place buy orders, anticipating a bounce, while others who are already in long positions might place their stop-losses just below this zone.)
/Pg 84 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2544 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (As the market approaches this level again, instead of holding, the price briefly dips below the support, triggering those stop-losses and activating pending orders. This sharp move below the level is not a true breakdown, but rather a strategic sweep to collect liquidity. Large market participants often exploit these zones to accumulate positions at more favorable prices, taking advantage of retail traders' stop placements.)
/Pg 84 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
