3335 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In the chart below, we first see a Bullish Fair Value Gap \(FVG\) created after a strong push upward. Normally, a bullish FVG acts as a support zone, helping price continue moving higher. It shows strong momentum from buyers.)
/Pg 138 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
3336 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (However, in this case, price fails to hold above the bullish FVG. Instead of respecting it, the market breaks down through the gap, signaling that buyers are losing control. This breakdown is the first clue that the bullish momentum is weakening.)
/Pg 138 0 R
/K [5 6]
>>
endobj
3337 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (When price breaks down the bullish FVG and and moves aggressively in the opposite direction, the bearish FVG is now considered an Inverse Fair Value Gap \(iFVG\).  The creation of the iFVG is important because it marks the first clear shift in momentum. The market has rejected the previous bullish move and is now showing strength to the downside. This structure often signals the start of a new trend in the opposite direction.)
/Pg 138 0 R
/K [7 8]
>>
endobj
